## Part 2 — TP-Link TL-SG1016DE Switch Configuration

The TP-Link TL-SG1016DE is a web-managed 16-port gigabit switch. All configuration is done through its web UI — there is no CLI. The switch distributes VLAN traffic from the MikroTik trunk to pve-nuc1, pve-nuc2, and pve-srv1, and mirrors all traffic to Security Onion for passive packet capture.

### Switch Port Assignment

| Port | Connected Device | VLAN Mode | VLANs |
|------|-----------------|-----------|-------|
| Port 1 | MikroTik ether9 (primary) | Tagged trunk | 10, 20, 40, 50, 99 |
| Port 2 | MikroTik ether10 (redundant) | Tagged trunk | 10, 20, 40, 50, 99 |
| Port 3 | pve-nuc1 | Tagged trunk | 10, 50 |
| Port 4 | pve-nuc2 | Tagged trunk | 10, 20 |
| Port 5 | pve-srv1 | Tagged trunk | 10, 20, 40, 50 |
| Port 6 | Security Onion mirror port | Mirror destination | All (mirror) |
| Port 7–16 | Future expansion | Unassigned | — |

> **Note:** Port numbers above assume a standard patch panel layout. Adjust to match your actual cabling. The important detail is which VLANs each port carries, not the specific port number.

> **Note:** pve-srv1 connects to both MikroTik ether4 directly (OPNsense WAN transit) and to the switch (VLAN distribution). The switch connection to pve-srv1 carries VLANs 10/20/40/50 so that traffic between VMs on pve-srv1 and VMs on the NUCs can flow correctly.

---

### Step 1 — Access the Switch Web UI and Set Management IP

Connect a PC directly to the switch on any unconfigured port. Navigate to the switch's current IP in a browser. Default credentials on a fresh TL-SG1016DE are `admin` / `admin`.

Before configuring VLANs, assign the switch a static management IP on VLAN 10 so it remains reachable after VLAN configuration is applied. This prevents locking yourself out.

1. Go to **System → System Info → Management VLAN**
2. Set Management VLAN to `10`
3. Set IP address to `10.10.10.2/24`, gateway `10.10.10.1`
4. Save — reconnect at the new IP after saving

---

### Step 2 — Enable 802.1Q VLAN Mode

The TL-SG1016DE supports two VLAN modes: MTU VLAN (simplified port-based) and 802.1Q VLAN (the standard tagged mode). 802.1Q is required here because multiple VLANs must coexist on trunk ports.

1. Go to **VLAN → 802.1Q VLAN**
2. Enable 802.1Q VLAN
3. Save

> **⚠ Warning:** Enabling 802.1Q VLAN mode resets any existing port-based VLAN configuration. Do this before configuring individual VLANs.

---

### Step 3 — Create VLANs

The switch must know which VLAN IDs exist before ports can be assigned to them.

1. Go to **VLAN → 802.1Q VLAN → VLAN Config**
2. Add the following VLANs:

| VLAN ID | Name |
|---------|------|
| 10 | Management |
| 20 | Internal |
| 40 | Personal |
| 50 | DMZ |
| 99 | Untrusted |

---

### Step 4 — Configure MikroTik Uplink Ports (Tagged Trunk)

Ports 1 and 2 connect to MikroTik ether9 and ether10 respectively. They must carry all five VLANs as tagged frames. For each VLAN entry, add both ports as Tagged members.

| VLAN | Port 1 (MikroTik ether9) | Port 2 (MikroTik ether10) |
|------|--------------------------|---------------------------|
| 10 | Tagged | Tagged |
| 20 | Tagged | Tagged |
| 40 | Tagged | Tagged |
| 50 | Tagged | Tagged |
| 99 | Tagged | Tagged |

---

### Step 5 — Configure Proxmox Node Ports

Each Proxmox node receives only the VLANs relevant to its hosted VMs. The Proxmox bridges on each node are already configured — the switch just needs to pass the correct tagged frames through.

**pve-nuc1** — hosts Kali (VM 301, VLAN 50) and REMnux (VM 302, isolated/no NIC)
- Edit VLAN 10 → set pve-nuc1 port as **Tagged**
- Edit VLAN 50 → set pve-nuc1 port as **Tagged**

**pve-nuc2** — hosts corp-dc (VM 201), corp-win10 (VM 202), corp-ubuntu (VM 203) — all VLAN 20
- Edit VLAN 10 → set pve-nuc2 port as **Tagged**
- Edit VLAN 20 → set pve-nuc2 port as **Tagged**

**pve-srv1** — hosts all Management and DMZ tier VMs (VLANs 10/20/40/50)
- Edit VLANs 10, 20, 40, 50 → set pve-srv1 port as **Tagged**

---

### Step 6 — Set Port PVID (Native VLAN)

PVID determines how the switch handles untagged frames arriving on a port. All Proxmox nodes send tagged frames, so this is primarily a safety setting to prevent unexpected behavior from any untagged traffic.

Go to **VLAN → 802.1Q VLAN → Port Config** and set PVID:

| Port | Connected Device | PVID |
|------|-----------------|------|
| Port 1 | MikroTik ether9 | 1 (default — trunk carries tagged only) |
| Port 2 | MikroTik ether10 | 1 (default — trunk carries tagged only) |
| Port 3 | pve-nuc1 | 10 (Management — Proxmox host traffic) |
| Port 4 | pve-nuc2 | 10 (Management — Proxmox host traffic) |
| Port 5 | pve-srv1 | 10 (Management — Proxmox host traffic) |

---

### Step 7 — Configure Port Mirroring for Security Onion

Security Onion (VM 103) performs passive traffic analysis using Zeek and Suricata. It needs to see a copy of all traffic crossing the switch — not just traffic destined for its own MAC address. Port mirroring (SPAN — Switched Port Analyzer) duplicates all frames from specified source ports to a dedicated mirror destination port.

The mirror destination port connects to the NIC on pve-srv1 mapped to Security Onion's second (monitoring) interface. This NIC has no IP address — it exists solely to receive mirrored frames. Zeek and Suricata listen on this interface in promiscuous mode.

1. Go to **Monitoring → Port Mirror**
2. Set Destination Port to the Security Onion mirror port (Port 6)
3. Set Source Ports to all active ports carrying lab traffic (Ports 1–5 at minimum)
4. Set Mode to **Ingress + Egress** (capture traffic in both directions)
5. Enable → Save

> **Note:** The mirror destination port (Port 6) should not be a member of any VLAN. It is a passive receiver only. Adding it to a VLAN would allow it to inject traffic, which is not the intent.

---

### Step 8 — Save Configuration

The TL-SG1016DE does not automatically persist configuration to flash. Always save explicitly after any changes — a power cycle without saving reverts all configuration.

1. Go to **System → System Tools → Config Save**
2. Click **Save Config**

> **⚠ Warning:** Save immediately after every change. A power cycle without saving will revert all VLAN and port settings.

---

